> (see http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:Swanson4715.jpg)

That image is probably historically fine, but for
the medieval era on which NetHack is mostly modeled,
a bit skimpy. The yew longbows the English used
against the French were quite a bit longer than the
archers were tall; you only have to be tall enough
to get the bottom half up to and including the grip
to the top of your head in use, so a person of the
age might have used a longbow up to about 180% of
their own height (but probably not, due to problems
carrying it _to_ battle). Still, a bow visibly
longer than the archer's height would seem in order.

While that image is in sight, notice too the length
of the arrow, which pretty much _must_ be at least
that size to function, and for weight, nearly at
most that size as well: the length from one
shoulder, where the pulling hand finds its natural
resting point, across the chest, to the heel of the
opposite hand where the bow grip rests, plus a
generous allowance of "tip" beyond the bow grip so
the arrow head doesn't slip behind the bow grip and
impale the grip hand while the hunter is pivoting to
follow prey in motion.

